WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:06.740
So in order to cook a food, it is necessary to heat

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.650
the inside of the food up to some critical temperature.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:13.400
And we discussed previously that that critical temperature for most foods,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.000
be it steak or tofu or whatever it is that you like to eat,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.926
happens to be in the range of 60 degrees Celsius or thereabouts

00:00:19.926 --> 00:00:21.800
because that&amp;#39;s where the proteins in the food

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.490
unfold and interact with each other to cause the food to become cooked.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.770
We pointed out that there is a paradox, which

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.080
is that, if you look at the recipes for cooking foods,

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.479
they tend to require temperatures that are much

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:36.090
above that critical temperature so that, when you cook a steak in the oven,

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.310
for example, you use a temperature which is-- I don&amp;#39;t know-- 180 degrees Celsius

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:44.030
or so even though you only want the inside of the steak to get to around 60

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.360
degrees Celsius.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.237
And then we, basically, explained or started to hint at the reason why.

00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:51.320
And the reason why is that it turns out that there

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.460
are two chemical reactions that need to occur in order for food to be cooked.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.380
One is the texture on the inside has to change,

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.620
and the other is that you need there to be browning reactions on the outside.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.380
And we discussed that those occur at around 120 degrees Celsius.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.870
So now we want to try to put all of this information

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.780
together and try to think through why it&amp;#39;s hard to cook a steak.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.680
So that is the subject of this discussion.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.040
If you don&amp;#39;t like steak-- I myself don&amp;#39;t eat steak-- then

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.550
you can really think about your favorite food-- why it is hard to cook a potato,

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:26.450
why it is hard to cook tofu, why it is hard to cook fish.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.140
And whatever it is that you like to eat, what I&amp;#39;m about to say applies.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:35.180
So you might, for example, choose to cook your steak at 180 or 190 degrees

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.270
Celsius.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.521
So we want the internal temperature, which starts out at room temperature,

00:01:39.521 --> 00:01:42.020
because your steak might have been resting on the countertop

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.650
before you put it in your oven.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.980
So maybe the initial temperature is, say, 23 degrees Celsius.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:50.150
You want to raise that up to about 60 degrees Celsius because that&amp;#39;s

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:51.540
when the steak is cooked.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:56.670
And you also want the outside to get to a temperature of 120 degrees Celsius.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.730
Now, the first problem that you have when you think about this--

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:03.470
and the problem is that we want to get the outside to 120 degrees

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.530
Celsius-- is that you&amp;#39;ll all recall that the boiling point of water

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:12.470
is at 100 degrees Celsius, and the major ingredient of steak is water.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:16.150
And so that means that, initially, you can&amp;#39;t get the steak above 100 degrees

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.950
Celsius because it&amp;#39;s made mostly of water,

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.840
and water-- the maximum temperature it can

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.800
get to is 100 degrees Celsius before it changes phases and actually becomes

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.250
a gas.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.945
So how are we going to heat the steak up to 120 degrees Celsius?

00:02:29.945 --> 00:02:31.820
The way we&amp;#39;re going to do that is we&amp;#39;re going

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.060
to have to evaporate off some amount of the water

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.520
around the outside of the steak.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.850
If we do that, and there&amp;#39;s no water on the outside,

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.950
so you imagine the whole edge of the steak has no water,

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:47.966
and only the inside of a steak actually has the water, then the outside,

00:02:47.966 --> 00:02:50.090
once it&amp;#39;s got no water, you can actually heat it up

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:52.631
to close to the temperature of the oven or whatever you want.

00:02:52.631 --> 00:02:55.920
So you can get it up to 120 or 180 degrees or whatever.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.510
So if you imagine the temperature profile across the steak--

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:02.829
and here we&amp;#39;ll draw it-- so at the outside of the steak, right outside,

00:03:02.829 --> 00:03:05.870
where the steak is, the temperature is the temperature in the oven, which

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.390
is, say, 180 degrees Celsius.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:11.510
At the surface of the steak, you&amp;#39;d like to get it to 120 degrees

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:14.690
or so, maybe 130, so that you get the browning reactions to be

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:16.700
how you like them to taste.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:20.030
There&amp;#39;s then going to be a very thin layer where the temperature goes down

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:23.420
from the 120, 130 degrees to the 100 degrees, which

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.070
is the boiling point of water, which is how hot the outermost water

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:28.450
layer of the steak will be.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:31.414
And then what you would like is for it to get to be 60 degrees

00:03:31.414 --> 00:03:33.330
or whatever your temperature is in the middle.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.780
And so you can imagine a curve, which basically goes down

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.370
to 60 degrees in the middle.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:41.150
Now, the problem is, if you achieve this target with the way that one typically

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.330
achieves it when one is cooking is one puts a thermometer in the center

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.600
and says, hello, the temperature is at 60 degrees Celsius,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.290
the steak is cooked, and you see that that&amp;#39;s true in the center of the steak,

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:53.910
but the whole outside of the steak is necessarily

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:55.910
at a temperature which is higher than 60 degrees

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.250
because, otherwise, you won&amp;#39;t hit the browning

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:02.060
reactions that you need on the outside to create the flavor molecules that we

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:02.700
want.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.590
And that, actually, in its essence, is one of the reasons

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:10.100
why it&amp;#39;s so hard to cook a steak.

